<HelpLayout title="Show me the data" description="Where Badger keeps your PRs, strength trends, training volume, and plateau detection.">
  <h1>Show me the data</h1>
  <p>This guide is a map of where Badger keeps its numbers, so you can find the chart you are after without hunting.</p>

  <h2>Your first 5 minutes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the <strong>Progress</strong> tab.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Overview</strong> view shows your weekly sets per muscle group against the minimum and maximum amounts research suggests (MEV and MRV), plus a heatmap of how often you train.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Exercises</strong> view lists your movements with their trends.</li>
    <li>Tap any exercise to open its estimated 1RM graph, its PR list, and its full history.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Worth knowing about</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plateau detection</strong> (Settings → Coaching, off by default) flags an exercise that has stopped progressing.</li>
    <li><strong>Muscle balance warnings</strong> (on by default) point out a muscle group getting too little work compared with the rest.</li>
    <li><strong>Per-category volume targets</strong> on the Overview card let you set your own weekly set goals.</li>
    <li><strong>Exercise linking</strong> ties variants together so they share PR history (for example a barbell and dumbbell version of the same press).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The 3 things to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>PRs and graphs can be kept per machine, so a number on one machine is not mixed up with another.</li>
    <li>Estimated 1RM uses the Epley formula, calculated from your logged weight and reps.</li>
    <li>Volume landmarks (MEV and MRV) are research-based ranges to aim for.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The numbers come to you</h2>
  <p>The summary after each workout shows your volume, any PRs you hit, and your total sets, so the most useful numbers reach you without a trip to the Progress tab.</p>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <p>Every progress feature is covered in <a href="/help/progress/">the progress help</a>. For plain-English definitions of RPE, RIR, 1RM, and deload, see the <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a>.</p>

  <p>Want the full picture? See <a href="/guides/overview/">everything Badger does</a>.</p>
</HelpLayout>
